<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uttarakhand</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Uttaranchal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uttarakhand is a state of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a>, with its capital at Dehradun. It was carved out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in 2000 and is made up of the historical regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a>. Its mountains hold the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badrinath">Badrinath</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedarnath">Kedarnath</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangotri">Gangotri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamunotri">Yamunotri</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uttarakhand.jpg" alt="Uttarakhand" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Hamilton · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanda_Devi_-_Hidden_Summit,_Uttarakhand_India_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>India’s constitution lists Sanskrit among the languages of the nation, and the states of <b>Uttarakhand</b> and Himachal Pradesh have made it a second official language.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Every year villagers in the Garhwal region of <b>Uttarakhand</b> perform the Pandav Lila, a ritual re-enactment of episodes from the epic through dancing, singing and recitation.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Katyuris emerged in the central Himalaya, modern <b>Uttarakhand</b>, around the 7th or 8th century, perhaps after the decline of the Kuninda Kingdom and other hill states.</blockquote><a href="/state/katyuri-kingdom">Katyuri kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Anglo-Nepalese War of 1814 to 1816 the region passed to British control and is now part of <b>Uttarakhand</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kumaon-kingdom">Kumaon Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>They emerged as a state in the foothills of modern Himachal Pradesh and <b>Uttarakhand</b> around the 2nd century BCE, after the fall of the Maurya Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuninda-kingdom">Kuninda Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>It is now in <b>Uttarakhand</b>; old Tehri town was submerged by the Tehri Dam.</blockquote><a href="/state/tehri-garhwal-state">Tehri Garhwal State</a></li></ul></section>
